# Valgrind suppressions for the constant-time probe (tests/ct/ct_probe.cpp).
# Every entry names one accepted report and why it is accepted; anything
# else memcheck reports fails the probe.
#
# The kcb2.local PASERK text of a secret key is written by the
# table-driven base64url encoder (detail/base64url.hpp), which indexes a
# 64-character alphabet with six secret bits at a time. memcheck reports
# the secret-dependent address, not a branch. The probe reaches it when an
# unsealed key computes its kcb2.lid. The alphabet spans at most two cache
# lines, so the leak is at most one bit per character at cache-line
# granularity; an arithmetic encoder for secret keys is the fix, and until
# then the report is accepted only on this path: the encoder called from
# paserk_encode with the wiped string type that only secret keys use.
{
   cb2 base64url alphabet lookup on the PASERK text of a secret key
   Memcheck:Value8
   fun:*b64*encode*
   fun:*paserk_encode<*ZeroizingAllocator*
}
